    "lawCode": "Add §201-h, Lab L; add §153, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5055\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the labor law and the civil service law, in relation to\nestablishing a racial equity, social justice, and implicit bias training\nprogram for all state government and private employees in the state\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would require a training program in racial equity,\nsocial justice, and implicit bias to all public and private employers in\nthe state.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the labor law by adding a new Section 201-h to create\na racial equity, social justice, and implicit bias training program for\nevery private employee to take.\n\nSection two amends the civil service law by adding a new section 153 to\ncreate a racial equity, social justice, and implicit bias training\nprogram for every government employee in the state.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation requires the Division of Human Rights, the Civil\nService Department and Department of Labor to collaboratively establish\na training program for civil service appointees on racial equity, social\njustice, and implicit bias. Implicit racial biases shape not only indi-\nviduals' cognition and attitudes, but also their behavior. While leaders\nat all levels of government may take actions to acknowledge or support\nracial equity and social justice in New York State, all civil servants\nmust be informed and prepared to understand systemic racism in our soci-\nety.\n\nAccording to the Ohio State University Kirwan Institute for the Study of\nRace and Ethnicity, implicit bias refers to the attitudes or stereotypes\nthat affect our understanding, actions, and decisions in an unconscious\nmanner. These biases, which can be both favorable and unfavorable, are\nactivated involuntarily and without an individual's awareness. These\nimplicit associations that individuals harbor in their subconscious\ncause them to have feelings and attitudes about other people based on\ncharacteristics such as race, ethnicity, age, and appearance. These\nassociations develop over the course of a lifetime through exposure to\ndirect and indirect messages. In addition to early life experiences, the\nmedia and news programming are often-cited origins of implicit associ-\nations. Implicit biases, explicit biases, and structural forces are\noften mutually reinforcing.\n\nAs convincing research accumulates, it is clear that educating our citi-\nzens on racism, equality, and bias is no longer an option--it is a\nnecessity. The training required under this legislation will ensure that\nall civil servants, who are the professional branch of government admin-\nistration, interact with and treat individual citizens receiving\nservices and their colleagues with the respect they deserve.\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.8521B of 2020\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the 180 days after becoming law.\n",
